## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `RunLoop` `while(true)` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| **Thread.Interrupt + Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _thread = new Thread(() => RunLoop(_cts.Token));
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        var waitHandles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, token.WaitHandle };

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until either the event is signaled OR cancellation is requested
            int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(waitHandles);

            if (index == 1) // token.WaitHandle fired — cancellation requested
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();       // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);  // wait up to 5s for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation guarantees exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **`Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** on .NET 6+ | It simply won't work — there is no fallback |
| **`Thread.Interrupt` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9)** | Even if it compiles today, it only works on threads in `WaitSleepJoin` state and is being removed |
| **`WaitOne()` ignores `CancellationToken`** | You *must* use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` — a plain `WaitOne` will never wake on cancel |
| **Race condition: multiple items enqueued before loop iterates** | The original code had this bug too — `Reset()` + single `Dequeue` could miss items. Consider draining the queue in a loop (see below) |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timer/callback resources. Always `Dispose()` it. |

## Optional: Fix the pre-existing race condition

If multiple items are enqueued between `WaitAny` returns and `Reset()`, only one gets processed per wake. Drain the queue instead:

```csharp
_newEvent.Reset();

lock (_lock)
{
    while (_queue.Count > 0)
        Handle(_queue.Dequeue());
}
```

## Even simpler: use `BlockingCollection<T>`

For new .NET 8 code, the entire class can be replaced with the built-in producer/consumer primitive:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(() =>
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        });
        _thread.Start();
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }
}
```

`BlockingCollection` handles all the synchronization, signaling, and cancellation internally — no manual `WaitHandle` or lock needed.
